/*  FUNCTION NAME:      GetVars
    DESCRIPTION:*//**Reads several named variables from a state as one row, in the order asked. Over a column of states, one row per state. A table (SplitState's debug view) passes through unchanged.*/
/*  RETURNS:            array: one row of the named variables' values (native types), or one row per state for a column of states
    PARAMETERS:
        state           (Required) Single state string, or a vertical column of states (e.g. a SCAN result).
        names           (Required) The variable names, in answer order: an array constant such as {"cash","crates"}, or a range of names.
    EXAMPLE:            =GetVars(SetVars("crates", 5, "cash", 25), {"cash","crates"}) -> {25, 5}
    NOTE:               What /State writes round a fold when answer outputs are ticked (spec 0018). An input whose first cell isn't a state (SplitState(..., 1)'s debug table, headers first) is returned as it is: the debug toggle works inside GetVars. An empty state reads as one too. Scalar variables only: an array value is padded with #N/A.
    REVISIONS:          Date        Developer   Description
                        2026-11-12  Claude      Initial version (#461)
*/
GetVars = LAMBDA(
//  Parameter Declarations
    [state],
    [names],
    LET(
    //  Procedure
        keys, TOROW(names),
    //  A state always holds CHAR(30) after each key. SplitState's debug table has more than one
    //  column, or (one variable) a header first: either way its first cell isn't a state.
        table?, OR(COLUMNS(state) > 1, ISERROR(FIND(CHAR(30), INDEX(state, 1, 1)))),
        result, IF(table?, state,
                DROP(REDUCE("", keys, LAMBDA(acc, key, HSTACK(acc, GetVar(state, key)))), , 1)
            ),
        result
)
);
